# Minimal reproduction for the panic in stream::sort (src/builtins/stream/sort.rs).
# ValueType::Float.is_comparable() is true (nothing excludes it), so a Float column
# passes sort's up-front "is this column comparable" check -- but f64::partial_cmp
# returns None for NaN, and sort's comparator closure used to treat that as impossible
# ("Unexpected sort failure") rather than as a normal, well-defined case.
#
# NaN should get a defined position instead: it always sorts as the greatest value
# (last when ascending, first when descending), regardless of which side of the
# comparison it's on.
list:of 3.0 $(float:nan) 1.0 | sort | echo

# -infinity and +infinity are ordinary, well-ordered values (not NaN), so they sort at
# the extremes as expected, still before NaN. +0.0 and -0.0 compare as equal (per
# param_partial_cmp, which just delegates to f64's own partial_cmp), so sort (a stable
# sort) leaves them in their original relative order rather than erroring or crashing.
list:of 3.0 $(float:nan) 1.0 (0.0 - $(float:infinity)) $(float:infinity) 0.0 (-0.0) | sort | echo
